{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Amazon ECR introduces archive storage class for rarely accessed container images - AWS","context":"Amazon Elastic Container Registry (ECR) has introduced a new archive storage class to help reduce storage costs for infrequently accessed container images. This new feature allows users to meet compliance and retention requirements while optimizing storage expenses. ECR's lifecycle policies now support archiving images based on last pull time, enabling automatic archiving based on usage patterns. Users can also archive images individually using the ECR Console or API. Archived images do not count towards the image per repository limit and are inaccessible for pulls but can be easily restored within 20 minutes via the ECR Console, CLI, or API. All archival and restore operations are logged through CloudTrail for auditability. The new ECR archive storage class is available in all AWS Commercial and AWS GovCloud (US) Regions. For pricing details, visit the AWS pricing page. To learn more, consult the documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2025/11/amazon-ecr-archive-storage-class-container-images/"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"EC2 Image Builder enhances lifecycle policies with wildcard support and simplified IAM - AWS","context":"EC2 Image Builder, a service offered by AWS for automating the creation, distribution, and management of customized Amazon Machine Images, has introduced two new features to simplify image management. Firstly, wildcard patterns are now supported in lifecycle policies, allowing users to manage images from multiple recipes within a single policy. This feature automatically applies lifecycle policies to all matching recipes, including new ones created in the future. Secondly, IAM role creation for lifecycle management has been simplified, with the console now automatically populating the required default permissions when creating a new role. Previously, users had to create separate lifecycle policies for each new recipe or manually configure permissions, making it difficult to manage image lifecycles at scale. These enhancements reduce setup time and potential configuration errors, enabling users to manage their image lifecycle more efficiently. Lifecycle Policies are available in all commercial AWS regions","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/02/ec2-image-builder-lifecycle-enhancements/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"aws-resilience-lifecycle","skill_description":"Guides the end-to-end AWS resilience lifecycle integrating Resilience Hub v2, Fault Injection Service, and Application Recovery Controller. Covers the Define → Test → Operate workflow: from policy creation through failure mode assessment, to FIS experiment validation, to ARC operational controls. Applicable when the user wants a complete resilience strategy, needs to connect findings to experiments to controls, or is planning a resilience program. Also applicable for the meta question of whether marking NGRH findings as resolved is enough, whether they are \"done\" after resolving findings, or how to validate findings before resolving them. Not applicable for resolving or remediating a specific individual finding (see resilience-hub-failure-mode-assessment), or when a single service is explicitly named (e.g. \"what FIS experiment should I run\").\n\n\nServices: resiliencehub, fis, route53-recovery-control-config, arc-zonal-shift\nTasks: configure, test, audit\nPersona: architect, sre, devops\nWorkload: resilience\n*","skill_name":"aws-resilience-lifecycle"},{"rank_order":4,"title":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle pauses actions on objects that are unable to replicate - AWS","context":"Amazon S3 Lifecycle has introduced a new feature that prevents expiration and transition actions on objects that have failed replication. This change is aimed at helping users coordinate replication configuration or permissions changes with actions defined in their lifecycle rules. Objects that fail to replicate due to incorrect permissions or replication configuration will no longer be expired or transitioned, even if they match a lifecycle rule. Users can correct the issue and use S3 Batch Replication to replicate the failed objects. Once successfully replicated, S3 Lifecycle will automatically process these objects according to the configured rules. This change applies to all existing and new S3 Lifecycle configurations across 37 AWS Regions, including AWS China and AWS GovCloud (US). The deployment is ongoing and expected to be completed in the coming days. For more information, visit the S3 Lifecycle documentation and S3 Replication troubleshooting documentation","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/03/s3-lifecycle-pauses-actions-on-objects/"},{"rank_order":5,"title":"AWS Config now supports 191 additional managed rules - AWS","context":"AWS Config has added support for 191 new managed rules across various services including Amazon Bedrock, Amazon SageMaker, Amazon ECS, Amazon EKS, Amazon RDS, Amazon Redshift, Amazon S3, and Amazon CloudTrail. These rules cover encryption, logging, public access, network security, data protection, and operational best practices. Users can deploy these rules individually or as part of a conformance pack. The new rules include ACM_CERTIFICATE_RSA_CHECK for AWS Certificate Manager, API_GWV2_ACCESS_LOGS_ENABLED for Amazon API Gateway, APPSYNC_AUTHORIZATION_CHECK and APPSYNC_LOGGING_ENABLED for AWS AppSync, and many more for other services. The expansion increases built-in governance coverage across AI workloads and core cloud infrastructure","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/07/aws-config-additional-managed-rules/"}]}}"}]}], "label": "Verify ECR lifecycle policy rule priority evaluation order and match behavior"}

